<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swedish East India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> was a chartered trading company founded in 1731 and based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. It traded chiefly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other Asian goods into northern European markets. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> note that, while commercially marginal in global terms, the company's trade connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to the wider Asian commerce of the eighteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swedish-east-india-company.jpg" alt="Swedish East India Company" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandiera_della_Compagnia_Svedese_delle_Indie_Orientali.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was the last of the prominent seafaring nations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to join the East India trade. An earlier attempt, a twelve-year privilege granted on 14 June 1626 by King Gustav II Adolf to the Flemish merchant Willem Usselincx, ended in political difficulties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, Sweden was impoverished and trade was seen as a way to rebuild it.</p><p>The venture became possible through foreign backing, above all from British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants shut out of their own countries' companies. The Scottish merchant Colin Campbell, formerly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostend-company">Ostend Company</a>, was the driving force, and the Swede of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> origin Henrik König fronted the application. The royal privileges, the First Octroi, were granted on 14 June 1731 for fifteen years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first ships were seized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and destroyed by the British and French, and the company avoided confrontation by concentrating on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The charter was renewed in 1746, 1766 and 1786. Under the third charter all 39 voyages went to Canton and none of the ships was lost; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> went to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1780, the price of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was practically the only supplier, and the profit of that charter was 58% higher than that of the previous two.</p><p>In the fourth charter no dividend was paid, because new British rules on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> imports ended the profitable smuggling of tea into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After 1804 no more ships sailed, and a shareholders' meeting on 13 December 1813 dissolved the company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a> made up about 90% of the cargo from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in an overview of 1774, much of it re-exported and smuggled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Porcelain accounted for about 5% of the value, and an estimated 50 million pieces were imported over the years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, furniture and arrack, the spirit used in Swedish punsch, were also brought home.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and arrack entered Swedish homes, and Chinese styles influenced architecture, notably the Chinese Pavilion at Drottningholm. The ship Götheborg sank in 1745 just off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> fortress at the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a> after returning safely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A replica, Götheborg III, sailed for China in October 2005 and arrived in July 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swedish East India Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Baltic trade in grain, timber, naval stores and manufactures remained dominated by Dutch and English merchants, and later efforts to win a larger share of long-distance commerce, such as the <b>Swedish East India Company</b> founded in 1731 after the imperial era had ended, met with only modest success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
